  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Piloting integrated conservation livelihoods approaches around Western Area Peninsula National Park.</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">This is a pilot project supporting two communities near the Western Area Peninsula National Park (WAP-NP), namely Big Water in the Western Rural Area and Aberdeen community in the Western Urban area, in piloting small-scale livelihood opportunities and raising awareness among local communities on the sustainable management of resources. 

The pilot activity will be done in collaboration with 2 partner organisations - one with conservation focus (Conservation Society of Sierra Leone) and one with a community development and livelihoods focus (Caritas Freetown), with support from a consultant and CAFOD&#x2019;s Environment Adviser. We hope that each can bring their own unique technical expertise and experience, to develop an integrated approach that combines environmental and development perspectives and practices. 

The project will promote evidence-based research, small-scale conservation livelihoods and awareness-raising/advocacy primarily.  Partners will undertake a combined participatory environmental conservation and livelihoods needs assessment, and to use this to identify viable and sustainable &#x201C;conservation livelihoods&#x201D; options for poor households in these communities. We expect that the project  has the potential to link conservation and livelihoods to generate net positive, measurable social and environmental outcomes alongside a financial return for poor/impoverished communities in the WAP-NP. The project approach is directly linked to CAFOD&#x2019;s Integral Ecology Programme Model, emphasizing the interconnectedness of people and their environment as discussed in the &#x201C;cry of the earth and the cry of the poor&#x201D; rooted from the statement of Pope Francis.</narrative>
  </description>
